/**
 * PROSTATE CANCER: THE PROGRAMMES THAT FAILED. Seventeen randomised phase 3 trials that did not work, with the figure
 * that killed each one and the publication it was read from. A spike that records only what worked is a brochure.
 *
 * Read together they say something the successes do not. Prostate cancer has defeated, in large randomised trials:
 * every checkpoint inhibitor tried in an unselected population (IMbassador250, KEYNOTE-921, KEYNOTE-641,
 * KEYNOTE-991, CA184-043); every anti-angiogenic strategy (CALGB 90401, COMET-1); both endothelin receptor
 * antagonists (SWOG S0421, ENTHUSE M1); both attempts to exploit a resistance chaperone (SYNERGY, AFFINITY); the
 * only trial to select men by androgen receptor splice variant (ARMOR3-SV); a Src inhibitor (READY); and two drugs
 * that improved radiographic progression-free survival and not survival (ELM-PC 4 with orteronel, 10TASQ10 with
 * tasquinimod). ERA 223 is the one that did active harm.
 *
 * The pattern in the immunotherapy failures is the one IMbassador250 measured directly: prostate tumours express
 * little PD-L1, carry few mutations and admit few T cells, and the benefit that appears in exploratory biomarker
 * subgroups has never survived a prespecified test. The pattern in ELM-PC 4 and 10TASQ10 is a lesson about
 * endpoints: radiographic progression-free survival can improve substantially while overall survival does not move
 * at all, and in prostate cancer, where men live for years after progression, that gap is wide.
 */

